in @prism
in @meta
in @glass
in @epistemologic
in @epistemologic/math/bundle
in @epistemologic/math/music
in @epistemologic/math/music/harmonic
in @epistemologic/math/music/interval

# @epistemologic/math/music/dissonance — the Helmholtz/Plomp-Levelt
# roughness curve as the formatter's discriminator; dissonance IS
# holonomy at the audible altitude; the substrate's pause-or-auto-apply
# decision substrate-declared.
#
# The third sub-shard in the audible-register triple. `harmonic.mirror`
# (commit b031c8db) landed the eigenvalues (ratios on the harmonic
# series, Pythagorean comma as the audible-altitude holonomy).
# `interval.mirror` (commit b8bdb728) landed the algebra (Z_12, the
# circle of fifths, the neo-Riemannian P/L/R involutions). This shard
# lands the LOSS FUNCTION those eigenvalues are scored against and the
# DISCRIMINATOR the formatter uses to read between multiple admissible
# morphisms. Eigenvalues + algebra without a loss-function are
# floating; a loss-function without a discriminator is silent; the
# triple closes here.
#
# === The bridge ===
#
# `bundle.mirror` declares: "Holonomy IS loss. Comprehension always
# costs something." `harmonic.mirror`'s `comma_as_holonomy` named the
# Pythagorean comma as the first measured residual at the audible
# altitude. But the comma is the residual of ONE specific loop (twelve
# fifths vs seven octaves). Every ratio in the harmonic-ratio space
# carries SOME residual against the consonant basis; the residual is
# graded, continuous, and psychoacoustically grounded. Helmholtz
# (1863) and Plomp & Levelt (1965) showed that this graded residual
# is the perceived roughness of a frequency ratio — the curve that
# peaks near the critical-band-width interval (~25\% of a critical
# band, near the tritone region) and drops to zero at small-integer
# ratios.
#
# `helmholtz` IS the bridge action that lifts a ratio into this
# psychoacoustically-grounded loss measurement. Once it is substrate-
# declared, every ratio carries a typed dissonance value, and the
# substrate's loss-vector altitude reads each ratio as a graded
# holonomy in the consonant-basis spectrum.
#
# === Dissonance IS holonomy: the audible-altitude identity ===
#
# `harmonic.mirror`'s `comma_as_holonomy` is the SINGULAR case (one
# residual ratio → one holonomy value). `dissonance_as_holonomy` is
# the GENERAL case (every ratio → graded holonomy via the Helmholtz
# curve). The two close the audible-register holonomy bridge:
#
#   - The Pythagorean comma is the residual of a closed loop.
#   - Helmholtz dissonance is the residual of a single eigenvector
#     against the consonant basis.
#   - Both are holonomy in `bundle.mirror`'s carrier; both decrease
#     monotonically under kintsugi (per `bundle.mirror`'s
#     "e^(n+1) < e^(n) IS monotonic decrease of D's Rayleigh quotient");
#     both are read at the audible altitude as roughness / discord.
#
# Per Magnot (2025), cycle-averaged holonomy IS the contextuality index
# κ. Dissonance IS the audible-altitude reading of κ: the substrate's
# measurement of how-far-from-its-own-attractor the current spectrum
# sits.
#
# === The formatter's discriminator (THE LOAD-BEARING TICK) ===
#
# Per `docs/specs/mirror-spectral.md` §2.2 and §4.7, the auto-apply
# boundary is structural, not policy:
#
#   - **Singleton consonance** — exactly one admissible morphism strictly
#     decreases the loss vector under the lexicographic ordering. The
#     formatter auto-applies.
#   - **Pareto-front** — multiple admissible morphisms exist; the
#     lexicographic ordering does not collapse them to a unique top.
#     The formatter PAUSES and emits a typed pause-token carrying the
#     pareto front; consent (Alex, a higher-temperature Fate tournament,
#     a peer's vote) breaks the tie.
#   - **No admissible morphism** — all candidates are dissonant beyond
#     the substrate's resolution. The formatter refuses; the imperfection
#     stays a kintsugi target for a later tick.
#
# `is_pareto` IS the discriminator action. It reads a set of candidate
# morphisms (each carrying its own dissonance reading) and returns the
# verdict at the substrate's three-state surface:
#
#   - `pass`                  — singleton consonance; auto-apply
#   - `partial(confidence)`   — pareto-front; pause + present to consent
#   - `failure(reason)`       — all candidates dissonant; refuse
#
# Once `is_pareto` lands, the formatter is designable at substrate
# altitude: `mirror kintsugi <file>` reads candidates, queries
# `is_pareto`, branches on the verdict. The substrate's pause-or-
# auto-apply decision IS substrate-declared.
#
# === Substrate-pull discipline ===
#
# Per `boot/std/number.mirror`, `ref` is the substrate's float-valued
# reference floor (already used by `harmonic.mirror`'s `cents() -> ref`
# and by `glass.mirror`'s `opacity.weight: f64` precedent). The
# Helmholtz-curve output is a continuous roughness measurement in
# [0.0, 1.0] (normalised by convention); `ref` accepts it cleanly.
#
# Substrate gaps surfaced honestly (each one a forward-promise to a
# later substrate-pull tick; none invented here):
#
#   - `pareto_set` carries a list of candidate morphisms but the
#     substrate has no "morphism" carrier yet. The carrier is named
#     `candidate` with a `ref` content-handle and a `dissonance` score;
#     the candidate's morphism content lives wherever `@mirror/spectral`
#     eventually puts it. The substrate names the shape; the realisation
#     lifts when `@kintsugi/consent` lands.
#   - `is_consonant` needs a threshold for the consonance/dissonance
#     verdict; the substrate has no `threshold` primitive at the floor.
#     The action signature declares the carrier shape; the realisation
#     layer parameterises the threshold per consumer altitude (refract
#     measures one; the formatter measures another; the audition lens
#     measures a third).
#   - The `holonomy` carrier from `@epistemologic/math/bundle` is
#     abstract; the lift from `dissonance` to `holonomy` rides the same
#     obligation-blocked discipline as `harmonic.mirror`'s
#     `comma_as_holonomy`.
#
# === Pre-AI prior art ===
#
# Every type and action below names an established music-psychoacoustic
# object older than the discipline of computer science:
#
#   helmholtz / dissonance       Helmholtz, H. (1863) Die Lehre von den
#                                Tonempfindungen. The roughness theory of
#                                consonance and dissonance.
#   Plomp-Levelt curve           Plomp, R. & Levelt, W. J. M. (1965)
#                                "Tonal consonance and critical bandwidth."
#                                Journal of the Acoustical Society of
#                                America 38, 548-560. The empirical curve
#                                relating ratio to roughness.
#   critical band / roughness    Fletcher (1940); Plomp (1976); the
#                                bandwidth within which two tones interact
#                                to produce roughness.
#   consonance / dissonance      Pythagoras (~530 BCE) → Aristoxenus
#                                (~330 BCE) → Galilei (1638) → Helmholtz
#                                (1863); the substrate's contribution is
#                                *typing* the graded measurement so the
#                                eigenboard reads it.
#   pareto front                 Pareto (1906) Manuale di economia
#                                politica; the multi-objective
#                                non-dominance set.
#
# The substrate's contribution is *typing* these so the eigenboard
# can reason about them. No invention.
#
# Spec context: docs/specs/mirror-spectral.md §2.2 (the auto-apply
#               boundary), §4.7 (the consent surface formalisation),
#               §5.1 (the dissonance sub-shard proposal).
# Substrate decisions: [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
#                      [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]],
#                      [[architecture-connes-spectral-triple]] (the
#                      audible register of (A, H, D); dissonance is the
#                      D's spectrum read against the consonant basis),
#                      [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]]
#                      (`helmholtz` is the lift from ratio altitude to
#                      dissonance altitude; `dissonance_as_holonomy`
#                      is the lift from audible altitude to bundle
#                      altitude),
#                      [[feedback-no-bare-types]] (dissonance is its
#                      own newtype over ref; pareto_set and candidate
#                      are their own carriers; no bare ref escapes the
#                      discriminator).
# Forward-promise: `@kintsugi/consent` (formerly
#                  `@mirror/spectral/consent`; migrated 2026-06-10)
#                  is the consumer of `is_pareto`'s verdict. When that
#                  sub-shard lands, it pulls this shard's `is_pareto`
#                  action and binds it to the `mirror kintsugi` driver's
#                  pause-or-auto-apply branch. This shard does NOT pull
#                  `@kintsugi/consent`; the substrate-altitude
#                  arrow points upward (consent consumes dissonance,
#                  not the other way around).
# Related shards: epistemologic/math/music.mirror (the family root),
#                 epistemologic/math/music/harmonic.mirror (the
#                 eigenvalue altitude; ratio + comma_as_holonomy),
#                 epistemologic/math/music/interval.mirror (the
#                 algebra altitude; interval + as_ratio),
#                 boot/std/epistemologic/math/bundle.mirror (the
#                 holonomy carrier; dissonance lifts into it),
#                 boot/std/epistemologic/math/spectral-triple.mirror
#                 (the (A, H, D) framing; dissonance IS D's residual
#                 against the consonant basis),
#                 glass.mirror (verdict; ref; the substrate floor).
# Path-namespace property: this file at
# `shards/epistemologic/math/music/dissonance.mirror` declares
# `@epistemologic/math/music/dissonance` as a species under the
# `@epistemologic/math/music` family.

# === The dissonance carrier ===
#
# A typed measurement of perceived roughness for a frequency ratio.
# The Helmholtz/Plomp-Levelt curve maps ratio space to a continuous
# roughness value, normalised by convention to [0.0, 1.0]:
#
#   0.0  — unison / octave / consonant small-integer ratio
#   ~1.0 — critical-band-width interval (~25% of a critical band;
#          the tritone region; maximal psychoacoustic roughness)
#
# The carrier carries:
#   roughness — the curve's value; substrate-floor `ref`.
#                (`ref` is `glass.mirror`'s float-floor; same
#                substrate carrier `harmonic.mirror`'s `cents()`
#                returns.)
#   partials  — the number of partials summed when computing the
#                roughness. Plomp-Levelt's full curve is the sum over
#                pairs of partials in the two tones' spectra; the
#                partial count names how deep the sum went. `u32` per
#                the substrate's kernel-primitive discipline.
#
# Substrate-pull-correct: `ref` accepts the Helmholtz curve output
# cleanly; no new substrate primitive needed at the floor altitude.
# The substrate's existing float-floor already carries `f64` weights
# in opacity_map (per `glass.mirror`); dissonance reuses it.
#
# Identity contract: two dissonance values are equal iff their
# roughness and partial counts both match. The realisation layer
# enforces float-equality discipline (epsilon-bounded by convention
# at the audible altitude; ~5 cents is the resolved threshold for
# trained listeners per `harmonic.mirror`'s cents commentary).
type dissonance = {
  roughness: ref,
  partials:  u32,
}

# === The candidate carrier ===
#
# A single candidate morphism with its dissonance reading. Used by
# the pareto-front discriminator to read a set of admissible
# morphisms scored against the consonant basis.
#
# Substrate gap (surfaced honestly): the substrate has no "morphism"
# type yet at the floor altitude. `content: ref` carries the
# substrate reference to wherever the morphism lives (a splinter
# oid, a shard handle, a Fate proposal id — the consumer decides);
# `score: dissonance` carries the discriminator's reading of how
# dissonant the candidate is against the current eigenboard state.
#
# When `@kintsugi/consent` lands (forward-promise), this
# carrier may lift to `candidate = { morphism: <typed-morphism>,
# score: dissonance }`. The substrate names the shape now so the
# discriminator can be declared; the typed-morphism lift waits for
# the consumer.
type candidate = {
  content: ref,
  score:   dissonance,
}

# === The pareto-set carrier ===
#
# The set of candidates the discriminator evaluates. Each element
# carries its own dissonance reading; the pareto-front question
# (`is_pareto`) asks whether the set has a strict minimum or whether
# multiple candidates tie at the dissonance floor.
#
# Substrate-pull discipline: this is `[candidate]` at the substrate
# altitude; the realisation layer enforces the contract that the
# list is non-empty when passed to the discriminator (the empty
# pareto set is the "no admissible morphism" case, which the
# discriminator returns `failure(reason)` for via its own input
# validation — named at the discriminator's signature, not at the
# carrier).
type pareto_set = [candidate]

# === The Helmholtz/Plomp-Levelt curve action ===
#
# THE LOAD-BEARING BRIDGE: ratio → dissonance at substrate altitude.
#
# Lifts a harmonic ratio (from `harmonic.mirror`) into a typed
# dissonance reading via the Helmholtz/Plomp-Levelt roughness curve.
# The curve is psychoacoustically grounded: small-integer ratios
# minimise roughness (the consonant basis); the critical-band-width
# interval maximises it (the tritone region); intermediate ratios
# grade continuously.
#
# Canonical values (Plomp & Levelt 1965; for normalised roughness
# at a fixed partial count):
#
#   1:1   → 0.00 roughness  (unison; pure consonance)
#   2:1   → 0.00 roughness  (octave; pure consonance)
#   3:2   → 0.02 roughness  (perfect fifth; near-pure)
#   4:3   → 0.05 roughness  (perfect fourth)
#   5:4   → 0.10 roughness  (major third)
#   45:32 → ~1.0 roughness  (augmented fourth / tritone; peak)
#
# Spectrally: `helmholtz` IS the eigenvalue → residual map at the
# audible altitude. Per `spectral-triple.mirror`, D's spectrum reads
# how far each eigenvector sits from the kernel; `helmholtz` reads
# how far each ratio sits from the consonant basis. The two are the
# same operation observed at two altitudes.
#
# The body stays obligation-blocked (`\`): the realisation lifts
# ratio terms through the Plomp-Levelt parametric form
# (g(x) = 4|x|e^(1-4|x|), summed over partial pairs); the substrate
# declares the bridge; the arithmetic lands when a consumer pulls.
helmholtz(r: ratio) -> dissonance { \ }

# === Bridge: interval → dissonance ===
#
# Composes through `interval.mirror`'s `as_ratio` and this shard's
# `helmholtz`. Reads a Z_12 interval, lifts to the just-intonation
# ratio, applies the Helmholtz curve. The chain that makes the Z_12
# algebra dissonance-readable.
#
# Audibly: `interval_dissonance(perfect_fifth_ascending) → ~0.02
# roughness`; `interval_dissonance(tritone_ascending) → ~1.0
# roughness`. Voice-leading constraints (the future `voice.mirror`
# sub-shard) read this action to enforce stepwise-motion preferences:
# a step has low dissonance; a leap has higher.
#
# Spectrally: `interval_dissonance` IS the composition
# `helmholtz ∘ as_ratio` — the algebra-altitude reading of the
# eigenvalue-altitude residual. After this action substrate-declares,
# every algebra element carries a typed residual against the
# consonant basis; the (A, H, D) triple is read end-to-end at the
# audible altitude.
#
# The body stays obligation-blocked (`\`): the realisation composes
# `interval.as_ratio(i)` with `helmholtz(r)`; both are substrate-
# declared in sibling shards.
interval_dissonance(i: interval) -> dissonance { \ }

# === Bridge: dissonance → holonomy ===
#
# THE AUDIBLE-ALTITUDE HOLONOMY IDENTITY.
#
# Lifts a dissonance reading into a `holonomy` value of the principal
# G-bundle (per `bundle.mirror`'s `type holonomy` carrier). After this
# action substrate-declares, the audible-altitude holonomy bridge is
# closed:
#
#   - `bundle.mirror` says:                "Holonomy IS loss."
#   - `harmonic.mirror`'s `comma_as_holonomy` says: "A comma IS a
#                                          holonomy." (one residual)
#   - This action says:                    "Dissonance IS a holonomy."
#                                          (graded; every ratio)
#   - Therefore:                           "At the audible altitude,
#                                          the substrate's loss carrier
#                                          IS the perceived roughness;
#                                          comprehension's cost has a
#                                          frequency, a graded
#                                          measurement, and a continuous
#                                          curve."
#
# The Pythagorean comma is the singular case (one closed loop's
# residual); Helmholtz dissonance is the general case (every
# eigenvector's residual against the consonant basis). The two
# together close the audible register's holonomy reading at
# substrate altitude.
#
# Spectrally: per `spectral-triple.mirror`, the bundle's `holonomy`
# is `terni::Metric` lifted from `terni::Loss` (per `bundle.rs`'s
# `Transport::Holonomy: Metric` post-audit). Dissonance is a non-
# negative, symmetric, triangle-inequality-respecting measurement of
# distance from the consonant basis — a `Metric` at the audible
# altitude. The lift IS substrate-fact: dissonance satisfies the
# Metric axioms by construction (Plomp & Levelt's curve is
# non-negative; symmetric on ratio-inverse pairs; triangle-bounded
# under composition).
#
# The body stays obligation-blocked (`\`): the realisation lifts
# `roughness: ref` through the holonomy carrier's concrete shape
# (itself abstract at `bundle.mirror`); the lift is constrained by
# the type system; the arithmetic lands when a consumer pulls.
dissonance_as_holonomy(d: dissonance) -> holonomy { \ }

# === The formatter's discriminator (THE LOAD-BEARING ACTION) ===
#
# THE PARETO-FRONT DISCRIMINATOR.
#
# Reads a set of candidate morphisms (each carrying its own
# dissonance score against the current eigenboard state) and emits
# the formatter's pause-or-auto-apply verdict. The three-state
# substrate floor (`verdict` from `glass.mirror`) carries the three
# branches the auto-apply boundary requires:
#
#   pass                  — singleton consonance; the pareto set has a
#                            strict minimum (one candidate's dissonance
#                            is strictly lower than every other's);
#                            the formatter auto-applies the winner.
#                            Per `docs/specs/mirror-spectral.md` §2.2:
#                            "loss-decreasing = auto."
#   partial(confidence)   — pareto front; two or more candidates tie at
#                            the dissonance floor (within the audible-
#                            altitude resolution of ~5 cents); the
#                            formatter PAUSES and emits a typed
#                            pause-token carrying the pareto set; the
#                            confidence value carries how close the
#                            top-tied candidates are to each other.
#                            Per §4.7: "the substrate emits pause(Φ)."
#   failure(reason)       — all candidates are dissonant beyond the
#                            substrate's resolution; no admissible
#                            morphism strictly decreases the loss
#                            vector; the formatter refuses; the
#                            imperfection stays a kintsugi target for
#                            a later tick. Per §2.2: "no admissible
#                            morphism" case.
#
# This action IS what makes the formatter designable at substrate
# altitude. `mirror kintsugi <file>` reads candidates from the
# kintsugi-tournament's gate+rank pipeline, queries `is_pareto`,
# branches on the verdict:
#
#   verdict = pass               → apply the winner; commit;
#                                  loss-decreased; tick advances
#   verdict = partial(c)         → emit `@mirror/spectral.pause(Φ)`;
#                                  yield to consent surface;
#                                  wait for resolution
#   verdict = failure(reason)    → leave imperfection; tick advances
#                                  to next focus; this fracture
#                                  awaits a later substrate state
#
# The substrate states the discriminator's shape; the realisation
# layer is the kintsugi driver wiring (a future `@mirror/spectral/
# consent` sub-shard, forward-promised but NOT pulled by this
# shard's `in @...` directives — the substrate-altitude arrow points
# upward; consent consumes dissonance, not the other way around).
#
# Per `bundle.mirror`'s level 4 closure: the discriminator IS the
# Lawvere fixed-point read at the audible altitude. The substrate
# settles when the discriminator returns `pass`; the substrate
# externalises when the discriminator returns `partial`; the
# substrate yields when the discriminator returns `failure`. The
# three-state surface IS the substrate's honest acknowledgment of
# its own observability boundary.
#
# The body stays obligation-blocked (`\`): the realisation reads
# the pareto set's dissonance scores, finds the minimum, compares
# the gap between minimum and second-minimum against the audible-
# altitude resolution (~5 cents per `harmonic.mirror`), emits the
# verdict. The substrate declares the discriminator; the arithmetic
# lands when `@kintsugi/consent` pulls.
is_pareto(candidates: pareto_set) -> verdict { \ }

# === The consumer-convenience threshold check ===
#
# A single-candidate consonance verdict. Returns the three-state
# substrate floor (`verdict`) based on whether the dissonance reading
# sits below the consonance threshold:
#
#   pass                  — roughness below threshold; the ratio is
#                            consonant (in the substrate's resolution).
#   partial(confidence)   — roughness near the threshold; the ratio is
#                            graded between consonant and dissonant
#                            (the mediant intervals; the borderline
#                            cases the curve resolves continuously per
#                            `harmonic.mirror`'s `is_consonant` shape).
#   failure(reason)       — roughness above threshold; the ratio is
#                            dissonant; it cannot consonate at the
#                            substrate's resolution.
#
# Substrate gap (surfaced honestly): no `threshold` primitive exists
# at the substrate floor; the realisation layer parameterises the
# comparison per consumer altitude (refract measures one threshold;
# the formatter measures another; the audition lens measures a
# third). The action signature declares the carrier shape; the
# threshold's concrete value lives at the consumer.
#
# Symmetry note: `is_consonant` here is the dissonance-altitude
# specialisation of `harmonic.mirror`'s `is_consonant(r: ratio)`.
# The two are the same verdict observed at two altitudes — ratio's
# consonance check is computable as `is_consonant(helmholtz(r))`.
# The substrate names both so the dispatch is honest at each
# altitude; the realisation layer composes them.
#
# The body stays obligation-blocked (`\`): the realisation reads
# `d.roughness`, compares against the threshold, emits the verdict.
is_consonant(d: dissonance) -> verdict { \ }

out @epistemologic/math/music/dissonance
out dissonance
out candidate
out pareto_set
out helmholtz
out interval_dissonance
out dissonance_as_holonomy
out is_pareto
out is_consonant
